 | `kms.touch` | `$BANANADS_TOUCH`, else `fe5e0000.i2c` | the bottom panel's touchscreen: the input device whose sysfs path contains this |
-| `kms.menu` | `on` | the in-game menu (src/menu.c: SuperDrastic's, in BananaOS's colours): L3 pauses the game and opens it on both panels. Resume, Save and Load (8 slots with pictures; a load can be undone), Quick settings (volume, brightness, the microphone's meter and a blow), Restart game, Quit game; in a local wireless game (`wifi.local`, another handheld in it) Save, Load and Restart game are off, as are F + R / F + L. `kms.menu_test=<n>` opens it 3 s in, on the main page (1), Save (2), Load (3), Quick settings (4) or the quit question (5), for tests |
+| `kms.menu` | `on` | the in-game menu (src/menu.c: SuperDrastic's, in BananaOS's colours): L3 pauses the game and opens it on both panels. Resume, Save and Load (8 slots with pictures; a load can be undone), Quick settings (volume, brightness, the microphone's meter and a blow), Restart game, Quit game; in a local wireless game (`wifi.local`, another handheld in it) the game keeps running behind the menu (`kms.menu_live`) and Save, Load, Restart game and Cheats are off, as are F + R / F + L. `kms.menu_test=<n>` opens it 3 s in, on the main page (1), Save (2), Load (3), Quick settings (4) or the quit question (5), for tests |
+| `kms.menu_live` | `on` | in a local wireless game the menu opens over the running game instead of pausing it (a paused handheld stops answering and the others drop it): the game gets no buttons and no touch, the top screen behind the menu keeps the frame from the moment it opened, the sound goes on; Resume, Quick settings and Quit game work, the rest is off. Once the local game has been over for 3 s the game pauses and the menu is the usual one. `off`: the menu pauses, as before |
+| `kms.menu_live_volume` | `50` | the game's sound while the menu is open over it, in % (100: unchanged) |
 | `kms.queue` | `2` | the most finished frames waiting for the screen (1-3); past that the oldest is dropped. Frames are committed one per refresh, so a game whose frames take 19 and 12 ms in turn still shows a new one at every refresh |
